12. UptimeRobot Monitoring

UptimeRobot is configured to monitor the deployed backend.

Website:

https://uptimerobot.com/

Monitored endpoint

https://multi-modal-rag-lyr4.onrender.com/

Current monitoring configuration
Monitor type: HTTP(s)
Endpoint: Backend root URL
Monitoring interval: 5 minutes
Reported status: Up
Why the HEAD request mattered

During setup, a health monitor encountered an HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed response when sending a HEAD request.

The backend root endpoint originally used:

python
@app.get("/")


The route was updated to accept both GET and HEAD:

python
@app.api_route("/", methods=["GET", "HEAD"])
def read_root():
    """A simple health check endpoint."""
    return {
        "status": "ok",
        "message": "Welcome to the RAG Multi-Modal API"
    }


This change was committed and deployed. The live endpoint subsequently returned HTTP 200 for both HEAD and GET requests, and UptimeRobot showed Up.

Manual health checks in PowerShell

Test HEAD:

powershell
Invoke-WebRequest `
  -Uri "https://multi-modal-rag-lyr4.onrender.com/" `
  -Method HEAD `
  -TimeoutSec 60


Test GET:

powershell
Invoke-RestMethod `
  -Uri "https://multi-modal-rag-lyr4.onrender.com/" `
  -Method GET


Expected results:

HEAD: HTTP 200
GET: JSON response containing status: ok

UptimeRobot verifies endpoint availability. It does not verify that PDF processing, Gemini generation, or document retrieval is working.

Free hosting limitation

Free Render web services may spin down after a period without inbound traffic. A subsequent request may experience a cold start.

An Up status in UptimeRobot does not guarantee that the application is permanently awake or that every application feature works.

