14. Troubleshooting Reference
Problem: PDF upload fails

Check:

Whether the backend is running.
Whether the frontend uses the correct API base URL.
Whether the browser's Network tab shows a failed request.
Whether the backend terminal reports an HTTP error or Python traceback.
Whether the uploaded file exceeds a configured request-size limit.

During local development, an earlier error showed:

POST /undefined/api/upload

This indicated that the frontend was constructing the request URL using an undefined value.

The frontend also reported a Next.js 1 MB Server Actions body-size limit. If this issue reappears, inspect the upload implementation and the actual failing request before changing size limits.

Problem: Gemini response generation fails

Verify that:

GOOGLE_API_KEY is present in the backend environment.
The key is valid and has the required access.
The configured Gemini model is available.
The backend logs show the actual exception.
ChromaDB retrieval returns relevant content.

The standalone Gemini test used during troubleshooting returned OK, confirming that a basic Gemini generation request worked with the corrected configuration at that time.

The application can still fail at later retrieval or response-generation stages.

Problem: Frontend cannot connect to the production backend

Check:

NEXT_PUBLIC_PYTHON_API_URL in Render.
Whether the frontend was rebuilt after the variable changed.
The browser's Network tab.
The requested API URL and response status.
The backend deployment logs.
Problem: Render cannot find requirements.txt

Confirm that the backend service uses:

python-ml-backend

as its Root Directory.

The correct backend build command is:

text
pip install -r requirements.txt


The correct start command is:

text
uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port $PORT

Problem: UptimeRobot reports HTTP 405

Test the deployed endpoint with both HEAD and GET. Confirm that the latest deployed code accepts both methods and that the Render deployment is Live.

Problem: ChromaDB telemetry warning

A telemetry message appeared in the logs:

Failed to send telemetry event ClientStartEvent

The application nevertheless started successfully in the observed local and production sessions. Treat this as a separate telemetry issue unless further logs show that it affects application functionality.

Problem: Frontend production build fails with a TypeScript ref error

A previous build encountered a mismatch between:

RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>

and:

RefObject<HTMLDivElement>

The prop declaration and the ref created with useRef need compatible types. Run npm run build after any related code change to confirm the fix.

15. Security and Repository Maintenance

Before publishing or updating the repository, verify the following.

Secrets
Keep .env and .env.local out of Git.
Keep the Gemini API key private.
Use Render environment settings for production secrets.
Never place secrets in public frontend variables.
Generated files

The following should generally not be committed:

Python virtual environments.
Node.js node_modules.
Next.js build output and caches.
Local ChromaDB database files.
Temporary backups such as *.bak.
Local credentials and private configuration files.

Ensure that .gitignore covers these items.

A .gitignore rule does not automatically remove files that are already tracked. Check the Git index when removing previously committed generated data.

Licensing and attribution

The project was originally obtained from another repository before being configured and modified locally.

Before removing inherited notices or publishing the repository as an independent project, verify the original license and any applicable attribution requirements. Keep notices that the applicable license requires.

Document your actual implementation, changes, and contributions accurately.

